# What NOT to send to the Docker daemon as build context.
#
# Without this file the context is the whole working tree — which here is
# tens of gigabytes, because `target/` is. Two consequences, one of which
# took down a pipeline:
#
#   * CI caches `target/` between jobs (see .gitlab-ci.yml). The image job
#     therefore starts with a populated target/, `COPY . .` writes it over
#     the one the build stage just produced, and the runner fills its disk.
#   * Locally, `just image` uploads the same tens of gigabytes to the daemon
#     before the build even starts.
#
# The build stage needs the workspace manifests and the sources. Nothing
# else here is read by any stage.

# Rust build output — regenerated inside the image, and the largest thing
# in the tree by orders of magnitude.
target/
.cargo/

# Version control.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# The documentation site: not compiled into the binary, and node_modules is
# large enough to matter on its own.
docs/

# CI and tooling config, read by the runner rather than by the build.
.gitlab-ci.yml
.github/
justfile
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Local environment. `.env` is gitignored, but a developer who has one must
# not have it baked into an image.
.env
.env.*

# Local data if the binary was run from the repo root.
atlas-data/

# Editor and OS scratch.
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
